---
export const prerender = true;

import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import TerminalWindow from '../../components/ui/TerminalWindow.astro';
import { portfolioConfig, primaryBenchmark } from '../../data/portfolio.config';

if (!portfolioConfig.enabled) {
  return Astro.redirect('/', 302);
}

const pct = (x: number) => `${(x * 100).toFixed(x * 100 < 1 ? 2 : 1)}%`;
const benchmarks = portfolioConfig.benchmarks;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Portfolio methodology"
  description="How the portfolio's returns, weights and costs are computed."
>
  <div class="mb-6">
    <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold flex items-center gap-2">
      <span class="text-terminal-accent-green">$</span>
      <span>Methodology</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="text-terminal-muted text-sm mt-2">
      How every number on the <a href="/portfolio/">portfolio page</a> is produced.
    </p>
  </div>

  <TerminalWindow title="methodology.txt" class="mb-8">
    <div class="prose prose-sm max-w-none">
      <h2>Sizes are weights, not amounts</h2>
      <p>
        A decision in the log records the <strong>weights it produced</strong> — “after this,
        the position is 8% of the book” — and nothing else. No share counts, no prices paid,
        no fees in currency, no totals. The source of this site is public, and shares × price
        reconstructs a balance, so that information is never written down in the first place.
      </p>
      <p>
        Between decisions, weights drift with prices. On a decision date they snap to the
        values declared in that entry. A weight path plus a price history is a complete
        description of the portfolio up to one unknown scale factor — and the scale is
        precisely the part being withheld.
      </p>

      <h2>Return is time-weighted</h2>
      <p>
        The headline number is the <strong>time-weighted return</strong> (TWR). Each trading
        day, the book's return is the weighted sum of its holdings' returns:
      </p>
      <pre><code>r_p(t) = Σ_i w_i(t−1) · r_i(t)</code></pre>
      <p>
        and the cumulative figure is the compounded chain of those daily returns:
        <code>Π (1 + r_p(t)) − 1</code>. Weights are carried forward and renormalised as
        prices move, and reset to the declared weights on each decision date.
      </p>
      <p>
        TWR is <em>scale-invariant</em>: it depends only on the weight path, not on how much
        money was in the account. Publishing weights therefore costs nothing in accuracy — the
        track record here is the real one, not an approximation of one. It is also the measure
        that isolates the decisions from the timing of deposits, which is what a log of
        reasoning should be judged on.
      </p>
      <p>
        Everything else — annualised return, drawdowns, best and worst day, hit rate, and each
        holding's contribution — is derived from that same daily series. Contributions are
        constructed so that they sum to the cumulative TWR rather than being computed
        independently. Volatility is the annualised sample standard deviation of daily
        <em>log</em> returns (they are the additive ones, which is what √252 scaling assumes),
        and Sharpe divides the same series' mean excess over a risk-free rate of
        {pct(portfolioConfig.riskFreeRate)} a year by it.
      </p>

      <h2>Prices are total return</h2>
      <p>
        Instrument returns come from <strong>dividend- and split-adjusted daily closes</strong>.
        Distributions are therefore already inside every number on the page; they are never
        modelled, added or reinvested separately, because doing so would count them twice.
        Only ratios between two dates are used — the level of an adjusted series has no
        meaning on its own.
      </p>
      <p>
        Everything is expressed in {portfolioConfig.baseCurrency}. Instruments quoted in
        another currency are converted with the daily FX rate on both endpoints, so currency
        moves are part of the reported return. Non-trading days are forward-filled; a
        benchmark or instrument with no data for a window simply reports nothing for it rather
        than guessing.
      </p>

      <h2>Trading costs</h2>
      <p>
        Every decision is charged a cost in <strong>basis points of turnover</strong> —
        {portfolioConfig.costBps} bp by default ({pct(portfolioConfig.costBps / 10_000)} of the
        weight actually moved), overridable per entry with <code>cost_bps</code>. Turnover is
        the one-way figure — half the sum of the absolute weight changes — so a decision that
        shifts 5% of the book from one holding to another is charged on 5%, not 10%.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is a cost in <em>return</em> terms, never a currency amount: a percentage of a
        percentage. It keeps the chart from flattering itself by trading for free, without
        revealing what a trade was worth.
      </p>

      <h2>Cash</h2>
      <p>
        The uninvested remainder is held as a reserved pseudo-ticker, <code>CASH</code>, so
        that “I went 30% cash” is expressible and scored fairly rather than silently
        disappearing from the denominator. It accrues the configured risk-free rate of
        {pct(portfolioConfig.riskFreeRate)} a year, accrued daily, and it is a real drag on
        return in a rising market — as it should be. Weights on any given day sum to 100%
        including cash.
      </p>

      <h2>Benchmarks</h2>
      <p>
        {benchmarks.length > 0 ? (
          <>
            Comparisons are computed over exactly the portfolio's own date range, from the same
            adjusted closes and in the same currency:{' '}
            {benchmarks.map((b, i) => (
              <>
                {i > 0 && ', '}
                <strong>{b.label}</strong>
                {b.symbol === primaryBenchmark?.symbol && benchmarks.length > 1
                  ? ' (primary)'
                  : ''}
              </>
            ))}
            . Excess return is the simple difference of cumulative returns. Beta, alpha and
            correlation come from daily returns over the same window.
          </>
        ) : (
          <>No benchmark is configured, so no comparison figures are shown.</>
        )}
      </p>

      <h2>What is not claimed</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>No money-weighted return.</strong> An XIRR needs dated cash flows in
          currency, which this log deliberately does not contain. Any number sensitive to
          <em>when</em> money arrived is therefore unavailable, and none is shown.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No profit or loss in euros.</strong> Realised and unrealised P&amp;L,
          position values and account totals are all amounts, and amounts are never published.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No tax, no interest on margin, no securities lending.</strong> None of it is
          modelled.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Weights are rounded.</strong> Entries record post-trade weights to about the
          nearest half percent, so the drift path between decisions is exact but its starting
          points carry that rounding.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Past returns are a record, not a forecast.</strong> Nothing here is
          investment advice.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </TerminalWindow>

  <div class="mt-8 text-center">
    <a
      href="/portfolio/"
      class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-terminal-muted hover:text-terminal-accent-cyan transition-colors text-sm"
    >
      <span>←</span>
      <span>Back to the portfolio</span>
    </a>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
